WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.005
Before 1996, a worker who changed jobs could face a new

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:10.010
waiting period for conditions she already had, and a small employer

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:15.015
could be turned down for coverage because one employee was sick.

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:19.110
The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act addressed both.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:24.115
It limited pre-existing condition exclusions in group plans and gave credit

00:00:24.115 --> 00:00:29.120
for prior coverage, required insurers to issue coverage to small employers

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.215
and renew group coverage, and created special enrollment rights.

00:00:33.215 --> 00:00:36.855
It also produced the privacy and security rules

00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:40.040
that govern how health information is handled.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:46.410
The Affordable Care Act later went further, but HIPAA’s framework remains on the exam.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.790
HIPAA forbids a group health plan from basing eligibility or premium contributions

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:56.721
on health status, medical history, genetic information, disability or claims experience.

00:00:56.721 --> 00:01:01.653
Everyone in the eligible class gets in on the same terms.

00:01:01.653 --> 00:01:06.136
HIPAA also created special enrollment periods: an employee who declined

00:01:06.136 --> 00:01:10.619
coverage may enroll midyear within 30 days after losing other

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:15.102
coverage, or after marriage, birth, adoption or placement for adoption.

00:01:15.102 --> 00:01:18.689
Later law added 60 days for gaining or

00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:22.275
losing Medicaid or Children’s Health Insurance Program eligibility.

00:01:22.275 --> 00:01:27.207
Priya’s employee who waived coverage because of a spouse’s plan may

00:01:27.207 --> 00:01:31.690
join within 30 days if the spouse loses that plan.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:35.768
HIPAA required insurers in the small group market, then defined as

00:01:35.768 --> 00:01:39.847
2 to 50 employees, to accept every small employer that applied

00:01:39.847 --> 00:01:43.555
and every eligible employee of that employer, regardless of health.

00:01:43.555 --> 00:01:48.004
It also defined eligible individuals, people leaving group coverage after at least

00:01:48.004 --> 00:01:52.453
18 months with no gap of 63 days or more who had

00:01:52.453 --> 00:01:56.902
exhausted any COBRA, and required that they be able to obtain individual

00:01:56.902 --> 00:02:01.352
coverage; Maryland met that duty through a state high-risk pool before 2014.

00:02:01.352 --> 00:02:05.801
The Affordable Care Act extended guaranteed issue to all comprehensive individual and

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:10.250
small group coverage, so today no one is denied for health reasons.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.741
Before the Affordable Care Act, HIPAA let group

00:02:13.741 --> 00:02:16.795
plans limit coverage for certain pre-existing conditions.

00:02:16.795 --> 00:02:18.977
The look-back was six months.

00:02:18.977 --> 00:02:25.085
An exclusion could last up to 12 months, or 18 for a late enrollee.

00:02:25.085 --> 00:02:29.448
Prior coverage could shorten that wait day for day if

00:02:29.448 --> 00:02:33.375
there was no break of 63 days or more.

00:02:33.375 --> 00:02:36.866
Plans issued certificates to document that creditable coverage.

00:02:36.866 --> 00:02:38.175
Pregnancy was protected.

00:02:38.175 --> 00:02:44.283
So were children enrolled within 30 days of birth, adoption or placement for adoption.

00:02:44.283 --> 00:02:48.647
The rule today is simpler: comprehensive group plans cannot exclude

00:02:48.647 --> 00:02:53.010
a condition just because you had it before you enrolled.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.846
HIPAA requires insurers to renew group and individual coverage at the policyholder’s option.

00:02:57.846 --> 00:03:03.054
Nonrenewal is allowed only for nonpayment of premium, fraud or intentional misrepresentation, failure to

00:03:03.054 --> 00:03:08.262
meet participation or contribution rules in a group plan, movement outside the service area,

00:03:08.262 --> 00:03:12.726
or the insurer’s withdrawal from the entire market with 180 days’ notice.

00:03:12.726 --> 00:03:16.818
An insurer may not drop Priya’s group because claims ran high.

00:03:16.818 --> 00:03:20.538
Premiums may still change at renewal within the rating rules.

00:03:20.538 --> 00:03:23.514
This is the guaranteed renewable clause from Lesson

00:03:23.514 --> 00:03:26.490
17 written into federal law for medical coverage.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:32.419
Think of HIPAA as the floor the Affordable Care Act built on.

00:03:32.419 --> 00:03:37.361
HIPAA said group plans could not pick and choose employees

00:03:37.361 --> 00:03:42.302
by health, had to credit prior coverage against limited pre-existing

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:47.243
exclusions, had to accept small employers and had to renew.

00:03:47.243 --> 00:03:51.690
The ACA removed pre-existing exclusions and health underwriting from

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:56.137
comprehensive coverage altogether and extended guaranteed issue to individuals.

00:03:56.137 --> 00:04:02.561
HIPAA’s special enrollment rights, renewability rules and privacy protections remain in force unchanged.

00:04:02.561 --> 00:04:07.502
When a question describes a group plan refusing a sick

00:04:07.502 --> 00:04:12.443
employee or excluding a condition for 12 months, the answer

00:04:12.443 --> 00:04:16.890
is that HIPAA, and now the ACA, forbid it.
